{"id": "b56a42e8-bd59-44ac-b2a3-4fd6f9f7e7f6", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll check the current lifecycle policy on `nebula-batch-worker` and cross-reference it against the images your running EC2 batch jobs are actually pulling, so I can tell you if anything in active use is at risk of expiring.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_2tGMzeqzHr6OD9WhaUjz6e", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Determine whether the ECR lifecycle policy on the repository 'nebula-batch-worker' is safe to leave as-is, specifically whether it could expire/delete any image that is currently in use by running AWS Batch jobs on EC2.",
  "context": "User runs AWS Batch jobs on EC2 compute environments that pull container images directly from the ECR repo 'nebula-batch-worker'. They want to confirm the existing lifecycle policy won't delete an image tag/digest that a currently running or recently-used job still depends on.",
  "resources": ["ECR repository: nebula-batch-worker", "AWS Batch compute environments/job queues/job definitions referencing this repo", "EC2 instances serving as Batch container instances"],
  "timeframe": "current state as of now",
  "criteria": "1) Retrieve the ECR lifecycle policy rules for nebula-batch-worker (tag status, countType, countNumber/countUnit, tagPrefixList, any rule priority). 2) List images in the repo with tags, push dates, and digests. 3) Identify which image tags/digests are referenced by AWS Batch job definitions (active revisions) and any currently RUNNING/RUNNABLE/STARTING batch jobs. 4) Simulate/reason whether the lifecycle policy's expiration rules (e.g., 'keep last N images' or 'expire images older than X days') would currently or imminently catch any image tag/digest still referenced by an active job definition or in-flight job. 5) Flag specifically if the policy expires untagged images only, or also tagged images, and whether 'latest' or floating tags used by Batch are protected by a tagPrefixList exclusion. Report back: the exact policy rules, the images currently in use, and a clear verdict on whether anything in active use is at risk, with specific image tags/digests called out if risk exists."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Check ECR lifecycle policy and cross-reference with images used by running Batch jobs on EC2."}], "created_at": null}

